        <li class="toc-h2"><a href="#errors">Errors</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>
  </div>

  <h2 id="quickstart">Quickstart</h2>
  <p>
    A point forecast is one request. Ask for the data product, a location, and the variables you
    want; omit <code>initTime</code> and you get the most recent model run in the archive.
  </p>
  <div class="frame">
    <div class="frameHeader">
      <div class="frameHeaderTitle">dynamical.org - API</div>
      <div class="frameHeaderSubtitle">Point forecast</div>
    </div>
    {% frameHighlight "bash", "frameContent" %}
curl -X POST 'https://api.dynamical.org/v1/forecasts' \
  -H 'content-type: application/json' \
  -d '{"queries":[{"dataProductId":"noaa-gfs-forecast",
                   "location":{"latitude":41.88,"longitude":-87.63},
                   "maxLeadTimeHours":6,
                   "variables":["temperature_2m","precipitation_surface"]}]}'
    {% endframeHighlight %}
  </div>
  <div class="try">
    <button type="button" data-try="forecast">run it</button>
    <span class="try-status"></span>
  </div>
  <pre class="frame frameContent try-output" hidden></pre>
  <p>
    The response pairs each variable's values with the axes they sit on. <code>validTimes</code> and
    <code>leadTimeHours</code> are the value axis; <code>initTime</code> is the run the values came
    from; <code>snapshotId</code> is the exact Icechunk snapshot that was read.
  </p>
  <div class="frame">
    <div class="frameHeader">
      <div class="frameHeaderTitle">dynamical.org - API</div>
      <div class="frameHeaderSubtitle">Response</div>
    </div>
    {% frameHighlight "json", "frameContent" %}
{
  "results": [
    {
      "dataProduct": {"id": "noaa-gfs-forecast", "version": "0.2.7"},
      "point": {
        "requested": {"latitude": 41.88, "longitude": -87.63},
        "selected": {
          "latitude": 42.0,
          "longitude": -87.75,
          "grid": {"latitudeIndex": 192, "longitudeIndex": 369}
        },
        "method": "nearest"
      },
      "forecasts": [
        {
          "initTime": "2026-08-05T12:00:00Z",
          "validTimes": ["2026-08-05T12:00:00Z", "2026-08-05T13:00:00Z", "…"],
          "leadTimeHours": [0.0, 1.0, "…"],
          "links": {"canonical": "/v1/data/noaa-gfs-forecast/FHKS78ERQ44CTNM127G0/points/192,369?…"},
          "data": {
            "temperature_2m": [22.75, 24.375, "…"],
            "precipitation_surface": [null, 8.678436279296875e-05, "…"]
          }
        }
      ],
      "variables": {
        "temperature_2m": {"unit": "degree_Celsius"},
        "precipitation_surface": {"unit": "kg m-2 s-1"}
      },
      "snapshotId": "FHKS78ERQ44CTNM127G0",
      "links": {"catalog": "https://stac.dynamical.org/noaa-gfs-forecast/collection.json"}
    }
  ]
}
    {% endframeHighlight %}
  </div>
  <p>
    From there: <a href="#list-data-products">list the data products</a> to see what is available,
    <a href="#list-forecast-runs">list runs</a> to pin an exact initialization time, or ask for
    <a href="#query-analyses">analysis</a> data to see what actually happened.
  </p>

  <h2 id="conventions">Conventions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Naming.</strong> Request and response fields are camelCase. Requests identify a
      product with <code>dataProductId</code>; a data-product resource calls its own identifier
      <code>id</code>. Variable names are the underlying dataset's, unchanged
      (<code>temperature_2m</code>).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Timestamps.</strong> ISO-8601, UTC, trailing <code>Z</code> on output. Timestamps you
      send must carry a timezone offset; a naive timestamp is rejected rather than assumed to be
      UTC.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Two time axes.</strong> A forecast response separates the run axis
      (<code>initTime</code>) from the value axis (<code>validTimes</code>, with
      <code>leadTimeHours</code> alongside). There is no synthesized issue time. Analysis responses
      have one axis, <code>analysisTimes</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Range boundaries.</strong> <code>initTimesAfter</code> and
      <code>initTimesBefore</code> are both exclusive. Analysis <code>startTime</code> is inclusive
      and <code>endTime</code> is exclusive. <code>maxLeadTimeHours</code> is an inclusive ceiling.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Latest run.</strong> Omitting <code>initTime</code>, or sending
      <code>"initTime": "latest"</code>, selects the greatest initialization time present in the
      opened snapshot. The response always reports the resolved timestamp — never the word
      <code>latest</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Nearest point.</strong> Locations snap to the nearest cell of the product's source
      grid and the response reports both the <code>requested</code> and <code>selected</code>
      coordinates plus the grid indices. A location outside the grid is an error, not a snap to a
      distant edge cell.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Batching.</strong> <code>POST</code> routes take a <code>queries</code> array and
      return a <code>results</code> array in the same order. Attach an optional
      <code>queryId</code> to a query and it is echoed on that result, so a batch stays unambiguous.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No echoed filters.</strong> Results do not repeat your request. What was resolved
      appears as data: the runs you got are listed in <code>forecasts[].initTime</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Missing values.</strong> A gap in the archive is <code>null</code>, never
      <code>NaN</code> — responses stay strict JSON.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Browsers.</strong> All public routes allow credential-free cross-origin access from
      any origin, and expose the headers a client needs to read: <code>ETag</code>,
      <code>Retry-After</code>, <code>Server-Timing</code>, <code>X-Dynamical-Work-Units</code>, and
      <code>X-Dynamical-Dataset-Cache</code>. Preflights are cacheable for a day, so a browser app
      pays that round trip once rather than per request.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="endpoints">Endpoints</h2>
  <ul class="index-list">
    <li>
      <a href="#list-data-products"><code>GET /v1/data-products</code></a>
