  <h3 id="list-data-products"><span class="method">GET</span> <code>/v1/data-products</code></h3>
  <p>
    Every data product the API can serve. The MVP catalog is the forecast and analysis products;
    <code>capabilities</code> says which point queries a product supports.
  </p>
  <div class="frame">{% frameHighlight "bash", "frameContent" %}
curl 'https://api.dynamical.org/v1/data-products'
  {% endframeHighlight %}</div>
  <div class="try">
    <button type="button" data-try="products">run it</button>
    <span class="try-status"></span>
  </div>
  <pre class="frame frameContent try-output" hidden></pre>
  <div class="frame">{% frameHighlight "json", "frameContent" %}
{
  "dataProducts": [
    {
      "id": "noaa-gfs-forecast",
      "name": "NOAA GFS forecast",
      "type": "forecast",
      "modelId": "noaa-gfs",
      "version": "0.2.7",
      "variableCount": 25,
      "capabilities": ["forecastTimeSeries"],
      "links": {
        "self": "/v1/data-products/noaa-gfs-forecast",
        "catalog": "https://stac.dynamical.org/noaa-gfs-forecast/collection.json"
      }
    }
  ]
}
  {% endframeHighlight %}</div>

  <h3 id="describe-data-product"><span class="method">GET</span> <code>/v1/data-products/{dataProductId}</code></h3>
  <p>
    The summary above plus what you need to build a query: every variable with its unit and
    dimensions, the dimension extents, the bounding box, and the license. An open-ended
    <code>extent</code> upper bound (<code>null</code>) means the archive is still growing along
    that dimension.
  </p>
  <div class="try">
    <button type="button" data-try="product">run it</button>
    <span class="try-status"></span>
  </div>
  <pre class="frame frameContent try-output" hidden></pre>
  <div class="frame">{% frameHighlight "json", "frameContent" %}
{
  "id": "noaa-gfs-forecast",
  "type": "forecast",
  "version": "0.2.7",
  "license": "CC-BY-4.0",
  "boundingBox": [-180.0, -90.0, 179.75, 90.0],
  "dimensions": {
    "init_time": {"type": "temporal", "extent": ["2021-05-01T00:00:00Z", null],
                  "unit": "seconds since 1970-01-01"},
    "lead_time": {"type": "other", "extent": [0, 1382400], "unit": "seconds", "size": 209},
    "latitude": {"type": "spatial", "extent": [-90.0, 90.0], "axis": "y",
                 "unit": "degree_north", "size": 721},
    "longitude": {"type": "spatial", "extent": [-180.0, 179.75], "axis": "x",
                  "unit": "degree_east", "size": 1440}
  },
  "variables": {
    "temperature_2m": {
      "dimensions": ["init_time", "lead_time", "latitude", "longitude"],
      "unit": "degree_Celsius",
      "longName": "2 metre temperature",
      "standardName": "air_temperature"
    }
  }
}
  {% endframeHighlight %}</div>

  <h3 id="list-forecast-runs"><span class="method">GET</span> <code>/v1/forecasts/runs</code></h3>
  <p>
    The initialization times actually present in the current snapshot, newest first, with the
    <code>snapshotId</code> they were read from. It reports what exists; it does not infer whether a
    run is pending or complete.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table class="data">
      <tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Type</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
      <tr><td><code>dataProductId</code></td><td>string</td><td>Required; must be a forecast product</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>initTimesAfter</code></td><td>timestamp</td><td>Exclusive lower bound</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>initTimesBefore</code></td><td>timestamp</td><td>Exclusive upper bound</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>limit</code></td><td>integer</td><td>1&ndash;100, default 20</td></tr>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="try">
    <button type="button" data-try="runs">run it</button>
    <span class="try-status"></span>
  </div>
  <pre class="frame frameContent try-output" hidden></pre>
  <div class="frame">{% frameHighlight "json", "frameContent" %}
{
  "dataProductId": "noaa-gfs-forecast",
  "snapshotId": "FHKS78ERQ44CTNM127G0",
  "runs": [
    {"initTime": "2026-08-05T12:00:00Z"},
    {"initTime": "2026-08-05T06:00:00Z"},
    {"initTime": "2026-08-05T00:00:00Z"}
  ]
}
  {% endframeHighlight %}</div>

  <h3 id="query-forecasts"><span class="method">POST</span> <code>/v1/forecasts</code></h3>
  <p>
    Point forecast time series, up to 10 queries per request. Each query selects one location, one
    data product, and one or more runs.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table class="data">
      <tr><th>Field</th><th>Type</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
      <tr><td><code>dataProductId</code></td><td>string</td><td>Required; must be a forecast product</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>location</code></td><td>object</td><td>Required; <code>latitude</code> &minus;90..90, <code>longitude</code> &minus;180..180</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>variables</code></td><td>string[]</td><td>Required; 1&ndash;25 names from the data product</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>queryId</code></td><td>string</td><td>Echoed on the matching result; 1&ndash;128 characters</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>initTime</code></td><td>timestamp or <code>"latest"</code></td><td>Defaults to the latest run in the snapshot</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>initTimesAfter</code><br><code>initTimesBefore</code></td><td>timestamp</td><td>A run range, both exclusive, sent together; mutually exclusive with <code>initTime</code>; at most 10 runs resolve</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>validTimeStart</code><br><code>validTimeEnd</code></td><td>timestamp</td><td>Trim the value axis; sent together, start exclusive of end</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>maxLeadTimeHours</code></td><td>integer</td><td>Inclusive ceiling on <code>leadTimeHours</code>; omit for every lead time the product publishes</td></tr>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Two runs of one product, trimmed to the first 6 hours, with per-query identifiers:</p>
  <div class="frame">{% frameHighlight "bash", "frameContent" %}
curl -X POST 'https://api.dynamical.org/v1/forecasts' \
  -H 'content-type: application/json' \
  -d '{"queries":[
        {"queryId":"chicago-12z",
         "dataProductId":"noaa-gfs-forecast",
         "location":{"latitude":41.88,"longitude":-87.63},
         "initTime":"2026-08-05T12:00:00Z",
         "maxLeadTimeHours":6,
         "variables":["temperature_2m"]},
        {"queryId":"chicago-recent-runs",
         "dataProductId":"noaa-gfs-forecast",
         "location":{"latitude":41.88,"longitude":-87.63},
         "initTimesAfter":"2026-08-04T00:00:00Z",
         "initTimesBefore":"2026-08-05T18:00:00Z",
         "maxLeadTimeHours":6,
         "variables":["temperature_2m"]}]}'
  {% endframeHighlight %}</div>
  <p>
    The second query returns one entry per resolved run in <code>forecasts</code> — that is how you
    compare successive runs at a point. Each entry carries its own
    <a href="#canonical-point">canonical link</a>.
  </p>

  <h3 id="query-analyses"><span class="method">POST</span> <code>/v1/analyses</code></h3>
  <p>
    Point analysis time series — the observed or reanalyzed record rather than a forecast. One time
    axis, <code>analysisTimes</code>, over a window you choose.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table class="data">
      <tr><th>Field</th><th>Type</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
      <tr><td><code>dataProductId</code></td><td>string</td><td>Required; must be an analysis product</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>location</code></td><td>object</td><td>Required</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>variables</code></td><td>string[]</td><td>Required; 1&ndash;25 names</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>startTime</code></td><td>timestamp</td><td>Required; inclusive</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>endTime</code></td><td>timestamp</td><td>Required; exclusive, after <code>startTime</code>, at most 31 days later</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>queryId</code></td><td>string</td><td>Echoed on the matching result</td></tr>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="frame">{% frameHighlight "bash", "frameContent" %}
curl -X POST 'https://api.dynamical.org/v1/analyses' \
  -H 'content-type: application/json' \
  -d '{"queries":[{"dataProductId":"noaa-mrms-conus-analysis-hourly",
                   "location":{"latitude":41.88,"longitude":-87.63},
                   "startTime":"2026-08-04T00:00:00Z",
                   "endTime":"2026-08-04T06:00:00Z",
                   "variables":["precipitation_surface"]}]}'
  {% endframeHighlight %}</div>
  <div class="try">
    <button type="button" data-try="analysis">run it</button>
    <span class="try-status"></span>
  </div>
  <pre class="frame frameContent try-output" hidden></pre>
  <p class="dim">
    The window the button sends is computed at click time — a date fixed in this page would fall off
    the end of the published record.
  </p>

  <h3 id="canonical-point"><span class="method">GET</span> <code>/v1/data/{dataProductId}/{snapshotId}/points/{gridIndices}</code></h3>
  <p>
    The immutable form of a point response. Every <code>POST</code> result hands you one of these
    URLs on <code>links.canonical</code>: the resolved snapshot, the resolved grid indices, and the
    exact query parameters that produced the values. Because the snapshot and the cell are pinned,
    the body can never change — it is served
    <code>Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable</code> and is the right URL to hand a
    CDN, a notebook, or a citation.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table class="data">
      <tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Where</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
      <tr><td><code>gridIndices</code></td><td>path</td><td>The two indices from <code>point.selected.grid</code>, comma-separated</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>variables</code></td><td>query</td><td>Required; comma-separated, up to 25</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>initTime</code></td><td>query</td><td>Required for forecast products</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>maxLeadTimeHours</code><br><code>validTimeStart</code><br><code>validTimeEnd</code></td><td>query</td><td>Forecast value-axis trimming</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>startTime</code><br><code>endTime</code></td><td>query</td><td>Analysis window</td></tr>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Snapshots roll forward as new data lands, so a canonical URL copied out of this page eventually
    stops resolving. The button below does it properly: one <code>POST</code>, then a
    <code>GET</code> of the canonical link that came back.
  </p>
  <div class="try">
    <button type="button" data-try="canonical">run it</button>
    <span class="try-status"></span>
  </div>
  <pre class="frame frameContent try-output" hidden></pre>

  <h2 id="ensembles">Ensembles</h2>
  <p>
    Products with an <code>ensemble_member</code> dimension return every member of the source
    archive, in order. The response replaces <code>data</code> with <code>ensembleMembers</code>;
    each entry keeps the archive's own <code>member</code> identifier and a <code>data</code> object
    with the same variable series a deterministic response would carry. Axes, units, and links are
    unchanged, so member 0 is read exactly like a deterministic forecast.
  </p>
  <div class="try">
    <button type="button" data-try="ensemble">run it</button>
    <span class="try-status"></span>
  </div>
  <pre class="frame frameContent try-output" hidden></pre>
  <div class="frame">{% frameHighlight "json", "frameContent" %}
"forecasts": [
  {
    "initTime": "2026-08-05T00:00:00Z",
    "validTimes": ["2026-08-05T00:00:00Z", "2026-08-05T03:00:00Z", "…"],
    "leadTimeHours": [0.0, 3.0, "…"],
    "ensembleMembers": [
      {"member": 0, "data": {"temperature_2m": [27.25, 25.0, "…"]}},
      {"member": 1, "data": {"temperature_2m": [27.125, 25.125, "…"]}}
    ]
  }
]
  {% endframeHighlight %}</div>
  <p class="dim">
    Members multiply the response: they count against the work budget below exactly as extra
    variables or lead times do, so trim the value axis when asking a 31-member product for a long
    horizon.
  </p>

  <h2 id="clients">Clients</h2>
  <div class="frame">
    <div class="frameHeader">
      <div class="frameHeaderTitle">dynamical.org - API</div>
      <div class="frameHeaderSubtitle">Python</div>
    </div>
    {% frameHighlight "py", "frameContent" %}
import httpx

response = httpx.post(
    "https://api.dynamical.org/v1/forecasts",
    json={
        "queries": [
            {
                "dataProductId": "noaa-gfs-forecast",
                "location": {"latitude": 41.88, "longitude": -87.63},
                "maxLeadTimeHours": 24,
                "variables": ["temperature_2m"],
            }
        ]
    },
    timeout=60,
)
response.raise_for_status()
forecast = response.json()["results"][0]["forecasts"][0]
for valid_time, value in zip(forecast["validTimes"], forecast["data"]["temperature_2m"]):
    print(valid_time, value)
    {% endframeHighlight %}
  </div>
  <div class="frame">
    <div class="frameHeader">
      <div class="frameHeaderTitle">dynamical.org - API</div>
      <div class="frameHeaderSubtitle">JavaScript</div>
    </div>
    {% frameHighlight "js", "frameContent" %}
