  <h2 id="bounds">Bounds and work units</h2>
  <p>
    A point request is cheap, but a careless one can ask for millions of values. These are the
    limits that keep the service predictable rather than a per-caller quota.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table class="data">
      <tr><th>Bound</th><th>Limit</th></tr>
      <tr><td>Queries per batch</td><td>10</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Runs resolved per forecast query</td><td>10</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Variables per query</td><td>25</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Span per analysis query</td><td>31 days</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Response values per batch</td><td>100,000</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Requests per minute, per client</td><td>1,200 (burst 120)</td></tr>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The work budget is the real size bound: values are counted across variables, times, runs, and
    ensemble members, and a batch that would exceed 100,000 is rejected rather than truncated. A
    successful response reports what it actually used in <code>X-Dynamical-Work-Units</code> —
    measure a small version of your query, then scale.
  </p>
  <p>
    The rate limiter is an overload guard, applied to the point routes and forecast-run discovery.
    Exceeding it returns <code>429</code> with a <code>Retry-After</code> header. Need more than
    this? Write to <a href="mailto:feedback@dynamical.org">feedback@dynamical.org</a> rather than
    working around it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="headers">Headers and caching</h2>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table class="data">
      <tr><th>Header</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
      <tr><td><code>X-Dynamical-Work-Units</code></td><td>Response values counted against the work budget</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>X-Dynamical-Dataset-Cache</code></td><td><code>hit</code> or <code>miss</code> — whether the archive was already open on the serving container. A batch that did both reports <code>hit|miss</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>Server-Timing</code></td><td>Per-phase timing: <code>catalog</code>, <code>dataset_open</code>, <code>point_select</code>, <code>query_select</code>, <code>zarr_load</code>, <code>response_build</code>, <code>json_encode</code>, <code>handler</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>ETag</code></td><td>Strong validator over the response body</td></tr>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="frame">{% frameHighlight "bash", "frameContent" %}
server-timing: catalog;dur=0.016, dataset_open;dur=1255.050, point_select;dur=0.076,
  query_select;dur=0.995, zarr_load;dur=668.879, response_build;dur=0.086,
  json_encode;dur=0.027, handler;dur=1925.222, dataset_cache;desc="miss"
x-dynamical-dataset-cache: miss
x-dynamical-work-units: 4
  {% endframeHighlight %}</div>
  <p>
    A cold <code>dataset_open</code> dominates a first request; the next request against the same
    product on the same container is far faster. Cache lifetimes follow how mutable the answer is:
  </p>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table class="data">
      <tr><th>Response</th><th>Cache-Control</th></tr>
      <tr><td>Data-product discovery</td><td><code>public, max-age=300, stale-while-revalidate=600</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Forecast-run discovery</td><td><code>public, max-age=30, stale-while-revalidate=120</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Point <code>POST</code> results</td><td><code>public, max-age=30, stale-while-revalidate=120</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#canonical-point">Canonical point</a></td><td><code>public, max-age=31536000, immutable</code></td></tr>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="errors">Errors</h2>
  <p>
    Errors are a JSON object with a <code>detail</code> string, or — for request-shape failures —
    FastAPI's validation array under the same key. Nothing is silently coerced: an unknown field, a
    naive timestamp, or a location off the grid is an error, not a guess.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table class="data">
      <tr><th>Status</th><th>When</th><th>Example detail</th></tr>
      <tr>
        <td>404</td>
        <td>No such data product</td>
        <td><code>Unknown data product 'nope'</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>422</td>
        <td>The request cannot be answered as written</td>
        <td><code>Location is outside the data product grid</code><br><code>Data product 'noaa-gfs-analysis' is analysis, not forecast</code><br><code>startTime must include a timezone</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>429</td>
        <td>Rate limit exceeded; honor <code>Retry-After</code></td>
        <td><code>Request rate limit exceeded</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>503</td>
        <td>The upstream STAC catalog is unavailable</td>
        <td><code>Failed to load STAC catalog …</code></td>
      </tr>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    A batch is all-or-nothing: if one query in <code>queries</code> fails, the whole request fails,
    so a <code>200</code> means every result is present.
  </p>
</div>

